          "tz": -480
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Di Wang",
          "email": "diwa@nvidia.com",
          "date": "2026-11-18 17:45:13.000000000",
          "tz": -480
        },
        "subject": "LU-21239 ldlm: mix all name words in the resource hash",
        "message": "LU-21239 ldlm: mix all name words in the resource hash\n\nldlm_res_hop_hash() sums the resource name words, so for a FID\nresource the hash is effectively seq + oid.  FID sequences are\nconsecutive and each restarts at a small oid, so resources from\ndifferent sequences collide, and only a small part of the 2^21\nMDT hash chains is used.  LU-8130 moved the MDT and MDC namespaces\nfrom a hash that mixed seq and oid to this plain sum.\n\nOn a busy MDS with 1.4M to 3.2M resources, ldlm_resource_get()\nwalked 16 to 100 entries per lookup under the bucket lock, and\nldlm_res_hop_keycmp() took about 10% of CPU per service thread.\n\nMix every name word with hash_64() and pick the chain with\nhash_32().  Only placement in the table changes.\n\nrsync test with 4096 workers on seven clients against a two-MDT\nfilesystem, patch on the MDS only (seconds, before -> after):\n\n                          MDT1 subdir       MDT0 root\nwarmup (all workers)       818.6 -> 729.2     778.4 -> 744.2\next4 -> Lustre update      366.7 -> 293.4     325.1 -> 296.3\nLustre -> ext4 scan         10.30 -> 3.98       7.71 -> 5.28\n\nldlm_res_hop_keycmp() falls from 7.19% to 0.22% of perf samples.\n\nFixes: d234e2cf5f55 (\"LU-8130 ldlm: separate buckets from ldlm hash table\")\nSigned-off-by: Di Wang <diwa@nvidia.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5\nChange-Id: I8a2a98f47cf50141453cea46eb4bd5f2806c20fd\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
